# Coding Style

## State and Mutation Boundaries (CRITICAL)

Prefer immutable values, isolated state generations, and detached published
snapshots. Do **not** apply a blanket "always create new objects, never mutate"
rule to every abstraction.

Use the contract of the abstraction:

```text
VALUE / VERSIONED SPECIFICATION
  prefer copy-on-write or persistent updates
  prior generations MUST remain unchanged

BUILDER / WORKSPACE / BUFFER / CACHE
  intentional local mutation is allowed when it is part of the documented API
  mutation MUST NOT leak across snapshot/publication boundaries

COMPILED / PUBLISHED / OBSERVED SNAPSHOT
  treat as immutable after publication
```

Examples:

```text
PerpetuaState.merge(delta)
  -> returns a deeply isolated next state; prior state remains unchanged

MiniGraph.add_node()/add_edge()
  -> intentionally mutate the construction builder and return self

MiniGraph.compile()
  -> produces a detached CompiledGraph snapshot; later builder mutations MUST
     NOT alter the compiled runtime

Future versioned GraphSpec.with_node()/with_edge()
  -> SHOULD use immutable/persistent copy-on-write semantics because graph
     definitions are versioned values, not construction workspaces
```

Rationale: immutability is valuable at value, version, publication, and
concurrency boundaries. Blanket prohibition of all mutation is not: it can
silently break established builder APIs and can force unnecessary copying.
Prefer the narrowest mutation scope that preserves explicit ownership,
isolation, deterministic behavior, and safe concurrency.

This section **supersedes** the previous always-applied rule:
`ALWAYS create new objects, NEVER mutate existing ones`.

## File Organization

MANY SMALL FILES > FEW LARGE FILES:
- High cohesion, low coupling
- 200-400 lines typical, 800 max
- Extract utilities from large modules
- Organize by feature/domain, not by type

## Error Handling

ALWAYS handle errors comprehensively:
- Handle errors explicitly at every level
- Provide user-friendly error messages in UI-facing code
- Log detailed error context on the server side
- Never silently swallow errors

## Input Validation

ALWAYS validate at system boundaries:
- Validate all user input before processing
- Use schema-based validation where available
- Fail fast with clear error messages
- Never trust external data (API responses, user input, file content)

## Code Quality Checklist

Before marking work complete:
- [ ] Code is readable and well-named
- [ ] Functions are small (<50 lines)
- [ ] Files are focused (<800 lines)
- [ ] No deep nesting (>4 levels)
- [ ] Proper error handling
- [ ] No hardcoded values (use constants or config)
- [ ] Mutation, if used, is intentional and bounded by an explicit ownership/snapshot contract
- [ ] Prior immutable/value generations remain isolated from later updates
